00:06:03.725 --> 00:06:07.400
- Yeah. So when the
Affordable Care Act

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:11.390
was put into place in about
2015, one of the things

00:06:11.390 --> 00:06:14.300
that was mandated there
is insurance companies can no

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:18.720
longer deny people coverage
for pre-existing conditions.

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:20.630
So it doesn't matter
what conditions you have.

00:06:20.630 --> 00:06:21.470
You have to get insurance.

00:06:21.470 --> 00:06:26.070
You can't discriminate based on rates
for those conditions that someone has.

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:29.720
So one of the things that is
in place in order to ensure that

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:32.330
happens is what's
called risk adjustment.

00:06:32.330 --> 00:06:34.250
And so throughout the year,

00:06:34.250 --> 00:06:37.700
all health insurance companies
submit their claims data and

00:06:37.700 --> 00:06:41.510
their diagnosis data for
their entire populations to these

00:06:41.510 --> 00:06:47.400
government-maintained servers. And then you
get credit for the risk.

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:50.120
So members have certain
conditions that counts them as

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:51.410
risky or not risky.

00:06:51.410 --> 00:06:53.810
There's a bunch of interaction factors
and a whole lot of math

00:06:53.810 --> 00:06:57.750
involved in calculating
how much risk a member has.

00:06:57.750 --> 00:07:01.280
And so then at the end of
the year is when the risk

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:02.490
adjustment takes place.

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:06.800
So insurers who insure a
riskier population actually get

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:10.370
transfer dollars from the
insurers who insure a less

00:07:10.370 --> 00:07:15.140
risky population. So it's
entirely budget-neutral. There's no

00:07:15.140 --> 00:07:17.540
government money involved
in changing hands.

00:07:17.540 --> 00:07:23.190
They facilitate that transfer between
those insurers, but not anything else.

00:07:23.190 --> 00:07:25.970
So if one insurer insures

00:07:25.970 --> 00:07:29.570
a bunch of elderly people
with multiple comorbidities and one

00:07:29.570 --> 00:07:32.660
insurer insures a bunch
of healthy college students,

00:07:32.660 --> 00:07:34.820
the one who insures a
bunch of healthy college students is

00:07:34.820 --> 00:07:38.810
going to owe money to that other
insurer at the end of the year,

00:07:38.810 --> 00:07:40.310
just to say

00:07:40.310 --> 00:07:42.260
we're covering more
risk than you are.

00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:46.610
And then there's the process-- so
I have a sister team who's

00:07:46.610 --> 00:07:49.280
involved in the risk
adjustment. And I

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:52.320
am in charge of the audit
of the risk adjustment.

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:54.200
So once you've submitted
all of this data,

00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:56.360
the government takes a
representative sample of your

00:07:56.360 --> 00:07:58.460
population and comes back
to you and says, prove it.

00:07:58.460 --> 00:08:02.790
Prove that these members have the
conditions that you say that they have.

00:08:02.790 --> 00:08:05.150
So we have to go and collect
medical records, talk to

00:08:05.150 --> 00:08:07.050
doctors, code all
of those records,

00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:11.000
and then recalculate the risk,
and prove that the population

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:14.240
distribution is as risky as we
said it was throughout the year.

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:19.590
- Interesting. Yes,
Libby said in the chat,

