I love what you said about
making sure that you know other

00:54:38.820 --> 00:54:42.840
teams exist across the
organization and figuring out

00:54:42.840 --> 00:54:45.840
what everybody
else is working on.

00:54:45.840 --> 00:54:48.570
And I was wondering, aside from

00:54:48.570 --> 00:54:50.130
the community event,

00:54:50.130 --> 00:54:53.190
how do you start to
build those relationships or figure out,

00:54:53.190 --> 00:54:55.470
in such a large organization,

00:54:55.470 --> 00:54:58.160
who you should talk to?

00:54:58.160 --> 00:55:00.310
- I think it starts small.

00:55:02.960 --> 00:55:04.220
When

00:55:04.220 --> 00:55:07.280
you're really close to a
data project and there's one piece

00:55:07.280 --> 00:55:10.190
that you don't know,

00:55:10.190 --> 00:55:14.930
it's like shooting the email to
the person that you saw last

00:55:14.930 --> 00:55:17.720
accessed that database because
you can check those things.

00:55:17.720 --> 00:55:21.800
You can say like, hey, I saw
you were using this data.

00:55:21.800 --> 00:55:25.310
What's going on with it? And
so those kinds of small emails--

00:55:25.310 --> 00:55:29.450
and it's also getting over
that hump of self-doubt,

00:55:29.450 --> 00:55:31.100
like, oh, am I emailing
the right person?

00:55:31.100 --> 00:55:32.990
Are they going to

00:55:32.990 --> 00:55:36.980
hate me for derailing their
day for emailing the wrong person?

00:55:36.980 --> 00:55:41.600
And just send an email because
the worst thing that happens is

00:55:41.600 --> 00:55:47.270
that they don't respond to you. And
then you send an email to another person

00:55:47.270 --> 00:55:48.320
and building that out.

00:55:48.320 --> 00:55:51.500
And then as I've moved through
into a leadership role,

00:55:51.500 --> 00:55:55.700
it's getting plugged in with
other team leaders, where they--

00:55:55.700 --> 00:55:57.830
OK. Now, I know the
name of your team.

00:55:57.830 --> 00:56:00.200
But do you have somebody on
your team who knows about this

00:56:00.200 --> 00:56:02.550
or who can help with this?

00:56:02.550 --> 00:56:05.000
And that's also
something that the

00:56:05.000 --> 00:56:08.690
Women in STEM group has helped
with because we have women in

00:56:08.690 --> 00:56:10.940
STEM across the
entire organization.

00:56:10.940 --> 00:56:15.170
They are not just in
corporate IT as your cloud engineers.

00:56:17.260 --> 00:56:20.050
Almost anyone with a data
analyst title or anything like

00:56:20.050 --> 00:56:21.310
that could count.

00:56:21.310 --> 00:56:25.930
And a lot of people in
health insurance have those math backgrounds.

00:56:25.930 --> 00:56:28.420
I've been exposed to so many
more people and so many more

00:56:28.420 --> 00:56:31.570
incredible women at Centene

00:56:31.570 --> 00:56:35.440
through that other organization that
now I know there's a new

00:56:35.440 --> 00:56:39.210
team, our member
insights team--

00:56:39.210 --> 00:56:40.640
brand new team, but I'm like,

00:56:40.640 --> 00:56:43.290
their manager immediately got plugged
in with Women in STEM.

00:56:43.290 --> 00:56:50.280
So now, I'm connected to her
on both pieces. So it's really great

00:56:50.280 --> 00:56:52.280
to be able to have that.

00:56:53.490 --> 00:56:56.130
- I love that.

00:56:56.130 --> 00:56:57.390
That's a great,

00:56:57.390 --> 00:56:59.580
great community anecdote there.

00:56:59.580 --> 00:57:02.520
It doesn't just have to
be the data science community where

00:57:02.520 --> 00:57:06.180
you are meeting all these
other people across different parts

00:57:06.180 --> 00:57:09.224
of the organization.

00:57:09.224 --> 00:57:12.760
I just want to double-check
with everybody here in the chat.

00:57:12.760 --> 00:57:15.580
I think there are a lot of
people who I think need to play

00:57:15.580 --> 00:57:18.430
some sort of game together
because there's a lot of talk

00:57:18.430 --> 00:57:20.430
about a certain game.

00:57:21.598 --> 00:57:24.880
But I just want to make
sure there are no other questions

00:57:24.880 --> 00:57:26.880
that I might have missed.

00:57:29.230 --> 00:57:35.890
One question I love to ask for
my own knowledge here is, if you think

00:57:35.890 --> 00:57:37.930
back to your

00:57:37.930 --> 00:57:43.060
career and as you're getting to--
gotten your role now, is there

00:57:43.060 --> 00:57:47.380
a piece of advice that comes
to mind that you received along

00:57:47.380 --> 00:57:51.205
the way or a favorite piece
of career advice that you've ever given?

00:57:54.980 --> 00:57:57.140
- I think

00:57:57.140 --> 00:58:02.720
it's something that, even coming
into this meeting, where I'm like--

00:58:02.720 --> 00:58:05.210
I'm great. And then I'm
also like, oh my goodness,

00:58:05.210 --> 00:58:07.210
what if people find
out I'm not great?

00:58:09.440 --> 00:58:11.900
That

00:58:11.900 --> 00:58:15.710
sort of imposter
syndrome, of just-- let

00:58:15.710 --> 00:58:19.250
people believe in you until
you can believe in yourself,

00:58:19.250 --> 00:58:22.640
where it's like no
one's giving you

00:58:22.640 --> 00:58:26.000
opportunities that they
don't think that you can do

00:58:26.000 --> 00:58:28.000
because everybody's
looking out for themselves.

00:58:30.200 --> 00:58:31.430
I've given that advice.

00:58:31.430 --> 00:58:34.220
I give myself that
advice because it is--

00:58:36.350 --> 00:58:38.780
let other people

00:58:38.780 --> 00:58:42.500
believe in you. And
I think that that's

00:58:42.500 --> 00:58:44.180
something that makes
the most difference.

00:58:44.180 --> 00:58:47.186
And it comes even
back around to

00:58:47.186 --> 00:58:49.380
if you think you're doing OK,

00:58:49.380 --> 00:58:54.570
send that email to the stranger and
hope that they can help you with

00:58:54.570 --> 00:58:56.520
your data.

00:58:56.520 --> 00:58:58.550
So it's just like, yeah,

00:58:58.550 --> 00:59:00.800
you probably have the
opportunities that you have

00:59:00.800 --> 00:59:02.800
because you earned them.

00:59:05.330 --> 00:59:09.920
- I absolutely love that.
Thank you so much, Katherine.

00:59:09.920 --> 00:59:11.810
If people want to get
in touch with you,

00:59:11.810 --> 00:59:14.330
is the best way LinkedIn?

00:59:14.330 --> 00:59:18.470
- It is. I am not generally
super active on posting

00:59:18.470 --> 00:59:23.240
on social media. But I am
a scroller. So if you reach out

00:59:23.240 --> 00:59:27.110
to me on LinkedIn,
I will get it.

00:59:27.110 --> 00:59:28.640
- Awesome. I just put it in,

00:59:28.640 --> 00:59:30.650
your LinkedIn there
in the chat as well.

00:59:30.650 --> 00:59:33.470
And it will be up
with the recording on the Data

00:59:33.470 --> 00:59:35.600
Science Hangout site. But
thank you so much,

00:59:35.600 --> 00:59:38.040
Katherine. This has been
a great conversation.

00:59:38.040 --> 00:59:40.520
And thank you for sharing
your experience with us all.

00:59:40.520 --> 00:59:41.960
- Yeah, thank you
for having me.

